diff --git a/docs/goose_spec.md b/docs/goose_spec.md
index 0f2ed1f9..a79dc2be 100644
--- a/docs/goose_spec.md
+++ b/docs/goose_spec.md
@@ -265,6 +265,19 @@ struct X { a: i8[3], b: i32, c: i64 = 0 }
   tail padding is added. In variable-class layouts (where offsets are dynamic)
   `pad n` still inserts n bytes, but bare `pad` has no defined alignment to
   aim for and inserts nothing.
+* `align n` among a struct's fields (n a power of two up to 64), or among an
+  enum's variants (up to 8), places every value of the type at an address
+  that is a multiple of n: in a variable, as an element of a fixed or
+  growable array, and on a data stack, whose top a growable array of such
+  values starts at is rounded up. It makes the type's C type aligned too, so
+  a value lent to C code compiled against an aligned struct (a Rust
+  `#[repr(C)]` one, say) is aligned as that code assumes. It adds no padding:
+  the type's size must already be a multiple of n, each field of an aligned
+  type must sit at a multiple of its own alignment in a type at least as
+  aligned, and an aligned type is at most 4096 bytes and is no element of a
+  limited or variable array, whose length goes before the elements. An
+  aligned enum's tag takes n bytes, so its payload starts at n: the layout of
+  a Rust `#[repr(C, u8)]` enum, given the same pads.
 * Fields are mutable by default; `let` before a field name means the field
   is not assigned after construction (§4.4), and `const f: T` is `let f:
   const T`: its contents are read-only too (§9.5).
